---
import MotionPage from '@/components/MotionPage.astro';
import MotionStage from '@/components/motion/MotionStage.astro';
import MotionUsage from '@/components/motion/MotionUsage.astro';
import MotionOptions from '@/components/motion/MotionOptions.astro';
import MotionTokens from '@/components/motion/MotionTokens.astro';
import Scopes from '@/components/motion/scenes/Scopes.astro';
import { ScopesControls } from '@/components/motion/scenes/ScopesControls';
import { chipClass } from '@/components/motionDemo';
---
<MotionPage
  slug="per-breakpoint"
  title="Motion: per breakpoint"
  description="Motion per breakpoint with --udx-motion and the Tailwind utilities motion-[name] and motion-none, so a phone and a desktop can play different motions. In HTML, React, Angular and Svelte."
  frameworkSwitch
>
  <Fragment slot="summary">
    Give an element a Tailwind class per breakpoint, such as <code>md:motion-[fade-on-view]</code>, to play another motion
    from that breakpoint up, or <code>motion-none</code> to play none.
  </Fragment>

  <MotionStage scene="scopes" label="Live example: Per breakpoint" height={[240, 408]}>
    <Fragment slot="controls"><ScopesControls client:visible size="page" /></Fragment>
    <Scopes size="page" />
    <Fragment slot="readout"><span class={`${chipClass} tracking-normal`} data-scopes-readout>Press Inspect this page to read the report of inspect().</span></Fragment>
  </MotionStage>

  <h2>Set a motion per breakpoint</h2>
  <p>
    A Tailwind class can set <code>--udx-motion</code> on an element to the name of a defined motion, or to <code>none</code>. Use the
    <code>motion-[name]</code> utility with any variant: <code>md:motion-[fade-on-view]</code> applies from the <code>md</code> breakpoint
    up, and <code>motion-none</code> turns the motion off.
  </p>
  <p>
    At each trigger (an entry, an exit, a replay, or a loop that starts or resumes), the element plays the named motion in place of its own.
    The named motion must be of the same kind: a reveal replaces a reveal, and a presence replaces a presence. A name of another kind, or one
    that is not defined, is ignored: the element plays its own motion, and a development build reports it once.
  </p>
  <p>The binding keeps its own triggers, and for a reveal, its own <code>axis</code> and <code>repeat</code>.</p>
  <p>
    In the example, the second card rises below <code>md</code>, and only fades from <code>md</code> up. The third card has
    <code>motion-none</code>, so it does not move.
  </p>
  <MotionUsage name="motion-scopes-breakpoints" live={false} />

  <h2>Options</h2>
  <MotionOptions
    rows={[
      { name: '--udx-motion', values: 'none · a defined motion of the same kind', default: 'the motion of the element', description: 'The motion the element plays in place of its own, at each trigger. none turns it off.' },
      { name: 'motion-[name]', values: 'a defined motion, in brackets, or motion-none', description: 'The Tailwind utility that sets --udx-motion. Put a variant in front: md:motion-[fade-on-view].' },
    ]}
  />

  <h2>Timing and tokens</h2>
  <p>The cards rise on the default spatial spring, and the fade that replaces a rise plays on the same spring.</p>
  <MotionTokens tokens={['spring.default.spatial']} />

  <h2>Reduced motion</h2>
  <p>
    Under reduced motion, a reveal shows its element at once, and so does each card of this page. With <code>--udx-motion: none</code> on the
    element or an ancestor, the element shows its end state without animating. See <a href="/motion/accessibility">Reduced motion</a>.
  </p>

  <p>
    The example's Inspect this page button reads the page with <code>inspect()</code>. To read what plays on a page, or to tear
    a section down, see <a href="/motion/inspect">Inspect and revert</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>If a card must play a presence, such as <code>fade</code>, name it in <code>data-motion</code> in place of the reveal: <code>--udx-motion</code> cannot change the kind of a motion.</li>
  </ul>
</MotionPage>
